##=================== /etc/anon/anonrc -- local/onion, B's side ==============##
#
# The onion SERVICE, and B's own way out. This daemon generates `connector-b`'s
# address, publishes a descriptor for it and forwards what arrives to the
# connector's one listener -- and it is the SOCKS5 proxy B reads A's
# self-description through when B's `POST /peers` names A's hidden-service URL
# (ADR 0075 decision 4: a peering is established from both ends).
#
# Mounted READ-ONLY at /etc/anon/anonrc; see `anonrc-a` for why the `Nickname`
# line below cannot be dropped.

# WITHOUT THIS THE DAEMON DOES NOT START -- see `anonrc-a`.
AgreeToTerms 1

User anond
DataDirectory /var/lib/anon
Nickname toonlocalonionB

ClientOnly 1
ORPort 0
DirPort 0
ControlSocket 0

# `connector-b`'s `socks_proxy`. See `anonrc-a` for the policy.
SocksPort 0.0.0.0:9050
SocksPolicy accept 10.0.0.0/8
SocksPolicy accept 172.16.0.0/12
SocksPolicy accept 192.168.0.0/16
SocksPolicy reject *

# ── The address, and where it lives ─────────────────────────────────────────
# `anon` writes the generated address to `HiddenServiceDir/hostname` and the
# private key beside it. THIS DIRECTORY IS INSIDE THE NAMED VOLUME compose
# mounts at /var/lib/anon, which is what ADR 0070's Consequences require: "if
# HiddenServiceDir is not on a persisted volume, the node's address changes on
# every restart and every counterparty's config goes stale silently". It
# belongs beside `state_dir` in an operator's head for exactly that reason.
#
# A SUBDIRECTORY of the DataDirectory volume rather than a second mount of its
# own, and that is an ownership argument, not tidiness: the image ships
# /var/lib/anon owned by its unprivileged user, so a fresh named volume
# mounted there inherits that ownership -- the same reason every connector
# here mounts a NAMED volume at /app/state rather than a host bind mount.
#
# The key is never committed and never leaves the volume.
# `tools/ci/check-tracked-secrets.sh` would refuse it, and a fixed onion key in
# a public repository is an address anyone could impersonate.
HiddenServiceDir /var/lib/anon/hidden_service

# ── A FIXED IP, and it has to be ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `anon` resolves this target when it PARSES its config, not when a stream
# arrives. A container NAME here aborts the daemon before it ever runs:
#
#     [warn] Unparseable address in hidden service port configuration.
#     free(): invalid size
#
# ...because `connector-b` does not exist yet, and cannot: its config names an
# address only THIS daemon can generate, so `local/keys.sh onion` starts the
# sidecars first and renders the address into that config afterwards (ADR 0070
# decision 7). The fixed IP breaks that circle, and `compose.yml` pins
# `connector-b` to it on the `onion-b` network. (The `free()` abort is an
# upstream bug in the error path -- the warning above it is the real message.)
#
# `local_topologies_load.rs` holds this literal and compose's `ipv4_address` to
# one string: an IP changed in one file and not the other publishes a
# descriptor for a port nothing is listening on, which arrives as a circuit
# that connects and then times out.
#
# ONE port for BOTH surfaces. That address is the single listener the connector
# binds: `GET /ilp/btp` upgrades the peering's websocket and `POST /ilp` serves
# the client edge A asks claim-state on. An onion address is a host, so it is
# addressed the way any other host would be -- port 80, and the paths decide
# the rest.
HiddenServicePort 80 172.31.0.10:3000

Log notice stdout
Log notice file /var/lib/anon/notice.log
